-- The no-per-module-type-namespace DESIGN POINT (widening slice 2, Task 3) --
-- documented as an intentional design fact, NOT a bug (see core-march-types.md
-- §2.5). Two sibling modules `A` and `B` each declare their OWN type named
-- `Foo`. Because types export/resolve by BARE name only, `A.Foo` and `B.Foo`
-- are literally the SAME nominal `TCon("Foo", [])` -- there is no per-module
-- type identity to keep them apart. `take_a` is annotated with the QUALIFIED
-- `A.Foo`, yet a `B.Foo` value (`B.make()`, a different constructor with a
-- different payload shape) is silently accepted with no diagnostic.
--
-- RECONCILED 2026-07-10 (post-merge of the order-independent-resolution work,
-- d95fe942): the two sibling types now use DISTINCT constructor names
-- (`MkA`/`MkB`). The original same-named-`Mk` version of this program no
-- longer typechecks -- NOT because per-module type identity appeared (it did
-- not; this program still passes), but because a same-named constructor in a
-- sibling module now shadows a module's OWN constructor inside its own body
-- (`Mk(1)` in `A.make` resolved against B's `Mk(String)`). That new
-- cross-module ctor-shadowing behavior is filed as its own finding in
-- specs/todos.md ("sibling-module constructor shadowing").
--
-- Value-witnessed: prints `7` -- proving the call actually typechecks AND
-- runs (not merely `--check`-clean), passing a `B.Foo`-shaped value where an
-- `A.Foo` annotation was written.
mod Main do
  mod A do
    type Foo = MkA(Int)
    fn make() : Foo do MkA(1) end
  end
  mod B do
    type Foo = MkB(String)
    fn make() : Foo do MkB("x") end
  end

  fn take_a(_x : A.Foo) : Int do 7 end

  fn main() do
    println(int_to_string(take_a(B.make())))
  end
end
